<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ogaden</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ogaden plateau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ogaden is a plateau and lowland region in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horn-of-africa">Horn of Africa</a>, today forming part of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. Its pastoral hinterland supplied livestock for export and recruits for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate</a>'s armies, drawn largely from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afar">Afar</a> nomads. The sultanate governed a confederation of pastoral and commercial communities across the Ogaden and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-aden">Gulf of Aden</a> coast.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ogaden.jpg" alt="Ogaden" width="320" height="202" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: United States. Central Intelligence Agency · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ethiopia,_the_Ogaden_Region._LOC_91682434.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The sultanate's pastoral hinterland, the <b>Ogaden</b> and surrounding lowlands, provided livestock for export and served as the recruitment base for its military contingents, drawn predominantly from Somali and Afar populations.</blockquote><a href="/empire/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…Liberation Front fought for Eritrean independence, rebels in Tigray included the nascent Tigray People's Liberation Front, and the Western Somali Liberation Front had by June 1977 driven the army out of much of the <b>Ogaden</b> into fortified towns before Somalia launched a full-scale invasion in the Ogaden War.</blockquote><a href="/empire/derg">Derg · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Rejected by local elites, he moved inland, won over clans in the Nugaal and <b>Ogaden</b>, and in 1899 declared a holy war, building a movement of followers called Dervishes (Darawiish).</blockquote><a href="/state/dervish-state">Dervish State</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1977 Somalia invaded Ethiopia's <b>Ogaden</b>, but the Soviets switched sides and Somali forces were defeated in 1978.</blockquote><a href="/state/somali-democratic-republic">Somali Democratic Republic</a></li></ul></section>
